{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-967ecb303e9ed9a9", "type": "tool_result", "content": "id=\"A1\" class=\"ltx_appendix\"> Appendix A Model and Training Details Our model comprises three components: an audio LM, a symbolic music LM, and a Q-Former connecting the two. This section provides detailed configurations of each component module and the training details. A.1 Q-Former The Q-Former is initialized with the MusicBERT-Base model [ 58 ] . The added cross-attention layers are randomly initialized. Following BLIP-2 [ 24 ] , we use K = 32 K=32 learnable queries, each with dimension 768. Symbolic piano arrangements are tokenized in the OctMIDI format [ 58 ] , which produces note-wise joint embeddings. Overall, the Q-Former comprises 186M parameters, including the learnable queries and note embedding layers. A.2 Audio LM We use MusicGen-Large [ 8 ] as our audio LM. We discard the text encoder and retain only the music decoder, a 48-layer Transformer. Audio codecs are fed to the decoder and we extract the hidden representations from the 25th layer, as prior probing studies [ 52 , 30 , 48 , 6 ] suggest that middle layers capture more musically meaningful features. This setup retains 1.7B frozen parameters from MusicGen. A.3 Symbolic Music LM For symbolic arrangement, we adopt MuseCoco-xLarge [ 29 ] , which is a 24-layer Transformer decoder pre-trained on large-scale symbolic music corpora. We remove its text-related components and keep 1.2B frozen parameters from the music decoder. Symbolic note tokens are converted into the REMI format [ 18 ] . Despite the slightly different tokenizations used across stages, we find that the latent representations learned in Stage-I remain compatible with Stage-II. To enable compatibility with MuseCoco, we project 𝐙 \\mathbf{Z} into the same embedding dimension as MuseCoco’s token embeddings via a linear layer. A LoRA adapter with rank 16 accommodates the added lead sheet condition. A.4 Training Details Model training focuses on the 186M Q-Former parameters, which is significantly smaller than the billion-scale LM backbones. In Stage-I, the Q-Former is pre-trained in FP16 using batch size 128 for 10 epochs (130K iterations). The LoRA adapter in Stage-II adds 5M parameters and we fine-tune the model for another 5 epochs using batch size 32. Both training stages are conducted on four NVIDIA A40 GPUs (48GB each). We use the AdamW optimizer [ 28 ] with an initial learning rate of 1e-4, a linear warm-up over the first 1K steps, and a cosine decay schedule to a final rate of 1e-5. At test time, we use top- k k sampling with k = 15 k=15 . Appendix B Objective Metrics We introduce five statistical metrics to evaluate content preservation and style coherence for the piano cover generation tasks. This section provides the definitions. B.1 Melody Chroma Accuracy (MCA) We use the 𝚖𝚎𝚕𝚘𝚍𝚢 . 𝚛𝚊𝚠 ​ _ ​ 𝚌𝚑𝚛𝚘𝚖𝚊 ​ _ ​ 𝚊𝚌𝚌𝚞𝚛𝚊𝚌𝚢 \\tt{melody.raw\\_chroma\\_accuracy} metric 2 2 2 https://mir-eval.readthedocs.io/latest/api/melody.html #mir_eval.melody.raw_chroma_accuracy provided by mir_eval [ 38 ] to evaluate the similarity between two monophonic melody sequences. For the reference melody, we apply Demucs [ 40 ] to isolate the vocal stem from the audio and then extract the F0 contour using pYIN [ 33 ] provided by librosa [ 34 ] . For the estimated melody, we obtain the melody skyline [ 47 ] from the generated piano cover MIDI and convert the MIDI pitches to frequencies. The two melodies are compared position-wise under a tolerance of 50 cents. While this implementation follows [ 42 ] , we additionally ensure that the two melodies are temporally aligned to the same sequence length. B.2 Chord Accuracy (CA) We introduce Chord Accuracy from [ 39 , 61 ] to measure the similarity between two chord sequences. For the reference sequence, when annotated chords are not available, we apply the method of [ 19 ] to detect chords from the input audio. For the estimated sequence, we use [ 20 ] to detect chords from the generated piano cover MIDI. Both chord sequences are aligned and compared at 1-beat granularity in terms of root and full quality based on the MIREX tetrads rule. 3 3 3 https://mir-eval.readthedocs.io/latest/api/chord.html #mir_eval.chord.tetrads B.3 Grooving Pattern Coherence (GPC) Grooving Pattern Coherence ( GPC ) evaluates the grooving pattern similarity between the generated piano cover and human’s arrangement. The grooving pattern , which is defined in [ 55 ] , represents the positions in a MIDI segment at which there is at least one note onset. We consider 4-bar segments at 1/4-beat granularity, deriving grooving pattern 𝐠 \\mathbf{g} as a 64-dimensional binary vector. The GPC over a test piece is defined as follows: GPC = 1 N ​ ∑ n = 1 N cos ⁡ ( 𝐠 n hm , 𝐠 n cv ) , \\mathrm{\\emph{GPC}}=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\cos(\\mathbf{g}^{\\mathrm{hm}}_{n},\\mathbf{g}^{\\mathrm{cv}}_{n}), (1) where N N is the number of non-overlapping 4-bar segments in the test piece. cos ⁡ ( ⋅ , ⋅ ) \\cos(\\cdot,\\cdot) computes the cosine similarity. 𝐠 hm \\mathbf{g}^{\\mathrm{hm}} and 𝐠 cv \\mathbf{g}^{\\mathrm{cv}} represent the grooving pattern feature from the human arrangement and the generated piano cover, respectively. The GPC metric is omitted for evaluation on Ballroom/GTZAN, where human’s piano arrangement is not available. B.4 Velocity Contour Coherence (VCC) Velocity Contour Coherence ( VCC ) evaluates the similarity of the velocity contour between the generated piano cover and the human arrangement, using the same formulation as GPC . We define the velocity contour as a time-series feature representing the average note velocity at each timestep. It has the same dimensionality and temporal granularity as the grooving pattern , but instead of a binary vector, it consists of real values ranging from 0 to 127. The VCC metric is omitted for Ballroom/GTZAN, where human’s piano arrangement is not available. B.5 Tempo Accuracy (TA) Tempo Accuracy ( TA ) evaluates the correctness of the estimated tempo tp est \\mathrm{tp}^{\\mathrm{est}} relative to the reference (ground-truth) tempo tp ref \\mathrm{tp}^{\\mathrm{ref}} . We first define correctness indicator D ​ ( k ) D(k) for one test piece as follows: D ​ ( k ) = 𝟙 ​ ( | tp ref − k × tp est | tp ref &lt; 0.08 ) , D(k)=\\mathbbm{1}\\left(\\frac{|\\mathrm{tp}^{\\mathrm{ref}}-k\\times\\mathrm{tp}^{\\mathrm{est}}|}{\\mathrm{tp}^{\\mathrm{ref}}}&lt;0.08\\right), (2) where 𝟙 ​ ( ⋯ ) \\mathbbm{1}(\\cdots) is the indicator function. k ∈ { 1 / 2 , 1 , 2 } k\\in\\{\\text{$\\nicefrac{{1}}{{2}}$},1,2\\} takes into account the octave ambiguity. The tolerance threshold of 0.08 follows the empirical setting in mir_eval. 4 4 4 https://mir-eval.readthedocs.io/latest/api/tempo.html #mir_eval.tempo.detection We then define tempo accuracy TA as: TA = max ⁡ ( D ​ ( 1 ) , 0.5 ⋅ D ​ ( 1 / 2 ) , 0.5 ⋅ D ​ ( 2 ) ) , \\mathrm{\\emph{TA}}=\\max\\left(D(1),\\;0.5\\cdot D(\\text{$\\nicefrac{{1}}{{2}}$}),\\;0.5\\cdot D(2)\\right), (3) where we consider half-tempo and double-tempo matches as partially correct (weight 0.5). This is because tempo perception is known to exhibit octave ambiguity, where perceptions at multiple metrical levels are still considered valid rather than true perceptual errors [ 10 ] . We derive the ground-truth tempo from beat annotations when available. On Ballroom/GTZAN, we use the audio tempo estimation results by madmom [ 5 ] , and we omit the A2M baseline in this setting because it already relies on madmom’s estimation in its pipeline. (a) The input lead sheet. (b) Piano cover based on the original soundtrack. (c) Piano arrangement in ragtime. (d) Piano arrangement in bossa nova. Figure 4 : Audio-to-symbolic arrangement for an 8-bar excerpt from The Sound of Music . Score is manually engraved from MIDI for visualization purpose. Figures 4(b) to 4(d) are arranged based on the lead sheet in 4(a) and an audio reference from the original soundtrack, a ragtime piece, and a bossa nova piece, respectively. Preserved music contents are highlighted in blue note heads. Synthesized audio is provided on the demo page: https://zhaojw1998.github.io/bossa/ . Ap\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "85cc878b-0f12-44c5-ab29-e82510ed1122", "uuid": "291a97c8-fc35-4162-8ece-a48da01bda5b", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T09:44:04.176Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "id=\"A1\" class=\"ltx_appendix\"> Appendix A Model and Training Details Our model comprises three components: an audio LM, a symbolic music LM, and a Q-Former connecting the two. This section provides detailed configurations of each component module and the training details. A.1 Q-Former The Q-Former is initialized with the MusicBERT-Base model [ 58 ] . The added cross-attention layers are randomly initialized. Following BLIP-2 [ 24 ] , we use K = 32 K=32 learnable queries, each with dimension 768. Symbolic piano arrangements are tokenized in the OctMIDI format [ 58 ] , which produces note-wise joint embeddings. Overall, the Q-Former comprises 186M parameters, including the learnable queries and note embedding layers. A.2 Audio LM We use MusicGen-Large [ 8 ] as our audio LM. We discard the text encoder and retain only the music decoder, a 48-layer Transformer. Audio codecs are fed to the decoder and we extract the hidden representations from the 25th layer, as prior probing studies [ 52 , 30 , 48 , 6 ] suggest that middle layers capture more musically meaningful features. This setup retains 1.7B frozen parameters from MusicGen. A.3 Symbolic Music LM For symbolic arrangement, we adopt MuseCoco-xLarge [ 29 ] , which is a 24-layer Transformer decoder pre-trained on large-scale symbolic music corpora. We remove its text-related components and keep 1.2B frozen parameters from the music decoder. Symbolic note tokens are converted into the REMI f